// EXPECT-DIAG: E130
// EXPECT-DIAG: Raw pointer type 'int*' cannot be used outside an 'unsafe' block
// Regression test (OVH-003, docs/planning/OVERHAUL_REMAINING_WORK.md):
// indexing a raw pointer-typed base (`buf.data[i]`, where `data: int*`) must
// be rejected outside `unsafe { }`/a raw-pointer-capability module, exactly
// like field/member access through one already is. Before this fix,
// TypeChecker::visitArrayAccess() never called checkUnsafeGate() at all --
// `l.data[100000] = 42;` compiled with no `unsafe { }` and no capability
// declared anywhere in this file, an out-of-bounds write reachable from
// ordinary safe code (F-PTR-1's "raw pointers are FFI-only" promise silently
// didn't apply to indexing). See raw_pointer_index_inside_unsafe_ok.tr for
// the corresponding passing case, and list_get_set_generic.tr for the safe,
// bounds-checked replacement (`List<T>.get()`/`.set()`) this file's uses
// should migrate to instead of reaching for `unsafe { }`.
//
// LEC-002: the pointer used to be `List<int>`'s own `data` field, which is
// private to list.tr now (and would fail HERE for that reason, not the one
// this test is about -- so this test declares its own struct). The struct
// itself sits in `unsafe { }` because a raw pointer field requires it.

unsafe {
    struct Buf {
        data: int*,
        len: int
    }
}

fn main(): int {
    mut b: Buf = Buf::default();
    b.data[100000] = 42;
    return 0;
}
